PUBLIC RESPONSE: {The core difficulty with the 201/301 proposals is that they apply the quota test after artificially separating the six fixed ballots from the rest of the profile. Under the standard non-strict Droop-style formulation used in the PJR and EJR definitions, the decisive test is whether any coalition N' satisfies |N'| ≥ ℓ · n/100 for the total electorate size n. Because the original six ballots are already present when the profile is assembled, they are absorbed into that calculation from the outset rather than treated as an increment that must be added on top. Consequently the 3-cohesive group on {a,b,c} meets the ℓ=3 line exactly at total size 200 (6 = 3 · 200/100). At that point any committee containing y and z already supplies the required intersection size for PJR; the remaining 98 seats can be filled with fresh candidates approved only by the 194 added ballots, none of which create new cohesive demands inside the forbidden set.  

The same absorption logic scaled to EJR’s stricter per-voter guarantee places the ℓ=2 cutoff at precisely n=300. Once the total reaches 300 the original group is no longer 2-cohesive, so a single representative from {y,z} suffices and the construction continues to work.  

Arguments that insist on a strict inequality therefore re-count the seed ballots inside every quota check—an operation the axioms themselves do not perform. The clean thresholds 200 and 300 are the first sizes at which every admissible coalition is satisfied while voter 1 remains unsatisfied, matching the quota-splitting conventions employed throughout the literature on both properties.}

